# The origin ARCANA accepts sign-in messages for.
#
# ONE FILE, BECAUSE THERE MUST BE ONE ANSWER. These values were written in three
# places — the agent-service unit, the web unit, and a literal repeated across
# eight verification suites — which meant changing the domain was an archaeology
# exercise and any place that was missed would sign for the wrong site and fail
# somewhere unrelated.
#
# Both systemd units read this file, and every verification run sources it. A
# sign-in message names the site asking for it and the server matches that name
# EXACTLY, so these must equal the origin the browser is actually on. The
# sign-in page compares them against its own host and refuses to sign when they
# differ, rather than putting a domain the visitor is not on into the text their
# wallet shows them.
#
# DOMAIN carries the host and port as the browser sees them, with no scheme.
# URI carries the full origin. For a public site on https these are
# `example.com` and `https://example.com`.
#
# If this file is absent the services still start: sign-in alone answers 503
# saying the domain is not configured, which is a loud, specific failure rather
# than a quiet acceptance of anything.
AUTH_SIWE_DOMAIN=arcana.local
AUTH_SIWE_URI=https://arcana.local
